    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The first great empire of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paithan">Pratishthana</a> (Paithan) and later from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> valley.</li><li>Its ports and inland towns traded with the Roman world, and merchants funded Buddhist caves at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karla-caves">Karle</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nashik">Nashik</a> and Kanheri.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gautamiputra-satakarni">Gautamiputra Satakarni</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-satraps">Western Satraps</a> c. 125 CE, but the empire fragmented in the early 3rd century.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Satavahana kings were known by their mothers' names, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gautamiputra-satakarni">Gautamiputra</a>, 'son of Gautami', and some of their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins bear legends in both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prakrit">Prakrit</a> and an early Dravidian language.</p></aside>
    </section>

