    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/simuka"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/simuka.jpg" alt="Simuka" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/simuka">Simuka</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Simuka_inscription_at_Naneghat_Caves.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Photograph by Kevin Standage Rubbing by J. Burgess (1883, d. 1892), CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Satavahanas rose in the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>, in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a> withdrew from the region in the second century BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a> call them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-region">Andhras</a>, list some thirty kings ruling for about four and a half centuries, figures generally considered too high, and name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simuka">Simuka</a> as the founder. Because the Puranas also credit the Andhras with ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanva-dynasty">Kanva dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a>, and because the earliest inscriptions and coins are hard to date, scholars place Simuka anywhere between the late third and the first century BCE. The family's origins are equally debated: the name Andhra later attached to the eastern Deccan, but the earliest Satavahana records come from the west, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naneghat">Naneghat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nashik">Nashik</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/junnar">Junnar</a>. Simuka was followed by his brother Kanha and then by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satakarni-i">Satakarni I</a>, the first great king of the line, who extended Satavahana rule over the western Deccan and probably into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>. His queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naganika">Naganika</a> recorded in the Naneghat cave inscription that he had performed Vedic sacrifices, including two horse sacrifices, or <em>ashvamedha</em>, and given lavish gifts to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a>. A Satakarni is also named as a western rival in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hathigumpha-inscription">Hathigumpha inscription</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharavela">Kharavela</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinga">Kalinga</a>, and an inscription on the southern gateway of the Great Stupa at Sanchi records a gift by a foreman of the artisans of King Siri Satakarni.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kalyan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kalyan.jpg" alt="Kalyan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kalyan">Kalyan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kalyan_Railway_Yard,_Kalyan,_Maharashtra,_India_-_panoramio_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Udaykumar PR, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Satavahana realm combined the black-soil plains of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millets</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pulses">pulses</a> were grown, with the river valleys of the Godavari and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> and the harbours of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konkan-coast">Konkan coast</a>. Its prosperity grew from trade. Passes through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ghats">Western Ghats</a>, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naneghat">Naneghat</a>, linked inland towns like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paithan">Pratishthana</a>, the modern Paithan, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ter-maharashtra">Tagara</a>, the modern Ter, with ports at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalyan">Kalyan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sopara">Sopara</a>, while the eastern territories opened on to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a>. The <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/periplus-of-the-erythraean-sea">Periplus of the Erythraean Sea</a></em>, a Greek merchant's guide of the first century CE, describes onyx, muslins and ordinary cloth carried from Paithan and Ter to the great port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bharuch">Barygaza</a>, modern Bharuch, for export, and Roman coins, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amphora">amphorae</a> and other imports have been found at Deccan sites. Merchants organised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a>, artisans, landowners and royal women made donations, recorded in hundreds of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prakrit">Prakrit</a> inscriptions, to Buddhist monasteries cut into the rock of the Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghat">Ghats</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karla-caves">Karle</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhaja-caves">Bhaja</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nashik">Nashik</a>, Kanheri and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/junnar">Junnar</a>; the Great Chaitya at Karle was the gift of a merchant, Bhutapala of Vaijayanti, and donors called <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yavana">Yavanas</a></em>, people of Greek or western origin, also appear. Society combined the Brahmanical norms that kings proclaimed with widespread support for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>. Kings were known by their mothers' names, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gautamiputra-satakarni">Gautamiputra</a>, son of Gautami, although succession passed in the male line.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/nahapana"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nahapana.jpg" alt="Nahapana" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/nahapana">Nahapana</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Silver_coin_of_Nahapana_British_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Uploadalt, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Little is known of the organisation of Satavahana armies, but inscriptions refer to victorious camps from which kings issued orders, and the realm relied on infantry, cavalry and elephants like other Indian states of the time. Its history was shaped by wars on several fronts. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharavela">Kharavela</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinga">Kalinga</a> claims in his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hathigumpha-inscription">Hathigumpha inscription</a> to have sent an army westward in defiance of Satakarni. The main rivals, however, were the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-satraps">Western Satraps</a>, Saka rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> who were probably at first subordinate to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire</a>. In the late first and early second centuries CE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kshaharata-dynasty">Kshaharata</a> ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahapana">Nahapana</a> occupied much of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nashik">Nashik</a> and the ports of the Konkan, as his family's inscriptions in the local caves show. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gautamiputra-satakarni">Gautamiputra Satakarni</a> reversed this in a campaign usually dated to around 125 CE, destroying the Kshaharata dynasty; a large hoard found at Jogaltembi near Nashik contains thousands of Nahapana's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins restruck with Gautamiputra's name. The recovery did not last. The Junagadh inscription of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudradaman-i">Rudradaman I</a>, the Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">Satrap</a> ruler of the Kardamaka line, dated 150 CE, boasts that he twice defeated Satakarni, lord of the south, but spared him because of their close relationship, a reference to a marriage alliance between the two houses. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yajna-sri-satakarni">Yajna Sri Satakarni</a> later regained some western territory.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gautamiputra-satakarni"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gautamiputra-satakarni.jpg" alt="Gautamiputra Satakarni" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gautamiputra-satakarni">Gautamiputra Satakarni</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coinage_of_Gautamiputra_Satakarni_with_Brahmi_legend.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rapson, E. J. (Edward James), 1861-1937, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Satavahana kings ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paithan">Pratishthana</a> in the west and, increasingly in the second century CE, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhanyakataka">Dhanyakataka</a>, near Amaravati on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>, in the east. They presented themselves as upholders of Brahmanical order: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nashik">Nashik</a> inscription of Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gautami-balashri">Gautami Balashri</a> praises her son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gautamiputra-satakarni">Gautamiputra Satakarni</a> as a unique <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a>, a destroyer of the Shakas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yavana">Yavanas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pahlavas">Pahlavas</a>, and a king who prevented the mixing of the four <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varnas</a></em>, while also recording gifts to Buddhist monks. The kingdom was divided into districts called <em>aharas</em>, such as Govardhana around Nashik, administered by royal officials known as <em>amatyas</em>, while great families with titles such as <em>maharathi</em> and <em>mahabhoja</em> governed parts of the realm as hereditary feudatories, often marrying into the royal house. Satavahana inscriptions contain some of the earliest known grants of land to Buddhist monks and Brahmins with immunity from royal officers and taxes, a practice that spread widely in later Indian states. The kings issued coins in lead, potin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and, later, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>; some silver portrait coins carry legends in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prakrit">Prakrit</a> on one side and in an early Dravidian language on the other. Prakrit was the language of administration, and royal titles and coin designs drew on both Indian traditions and the models of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-satraps">Western Satraps</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/karla-caves"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karla-caves.jpg" alt="Karla Caves" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/karla-caves">Karla Caves</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karla_caves_Chaitya.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kevin Standage (kevinstandage1@googlemail.com) INDIAN TRAVEL PHOTOGRAPHY, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Satavahanas created the first great state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> and gave the region a political identity independent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> valley. By uniting the western and eastern Deccan, they linked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a> ports, which traded with the Roman world, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> delta and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a>, from which trade and Buddhist teachers reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>. The rock-cut monasteries of their age at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karla-caves">Karle</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhaja-caves">Bhaja</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nashik">Nashik</a> and Kanheri, the earliest caves at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajanta-caves">Ajanta</a>, cut in the same period, and the sculpted stupa at Amaravati, enlarged and decorated in the late Satavahana period, are among the masterpieces of early Indian art, and the Amaravati style influenced Buddhist art in Sri Lanka and Southeast <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prakrit">Prakrit</a> literature flourished: tradition attributes to King Hala the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gatha-saptashati">Gatha Saptashati</a></em>, an anthology of seven hundred short poems of love and village life that shaped later Indian poetics, and places the lost <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brihatkatha">Brihatkatha</a></em> of Gunadhya, source of later story collections, at a Satavahana court. Their land grants, feudatory titles and royal ideology became models for the Vakataka dynasty, the Ikshvaku dynasty, the Pallava dynasty and other successors. The Satavahana period also spread coinage, writing and urban life widely across the Deccan, laying foundations for the kingdoms of the following centuries.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/rudradaman-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rudradaman-i.jpg" alt="Rudradaman I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/rudradaman-i">Rudradaman I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rudradaman_coin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Uploadalt, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The decline of the Satavahanas followed the defeats by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudradaman-i">Rudradaman I</a> and the gradual shift of the kingdom's centre to the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yajna-sri-satakarni">Yajna Sri Satakarni</a>, usually dated to the late second century CE, was the last strong ruler: he recovered parts of the western Deccan from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-satraps">Western Satraps</a>, as his inscriptions at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nashik">Nashik</a> and Kanheri show, and his coins circulated widely, some of them bearing the image of a ship that may reflect the importance of maritime trade. After his death the authority of his successors, among them Vijaya, Chandra Shri and a last Pulumavi, shrank rapidly, and by the early third century the kingdom had broken apart. Feudatories and local families took over its provinces. In the west the Abhira dynasty under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishvarasena">Ishvarasena</a> established itself around Nashik; in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> valley the Ikshvaku dynasty ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayapuri">Vijayapuri</a>, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagarjunakonda">Nagarjunakonda</a>, and continued the lavish patronage of Buddhist monasteries; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chutu-dynasty">Chutu dynasty</a> held the south-west around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banavasi">Banavasi</a>; and later the Pallava dynasty in the south-east and the Vakataka dynasty in the north inherited much of the former Satavahana sphere. The details of the collapse are poorly known, but the political and religious traditions of Satavahana rule, from land grants to the patronage of both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a> and Buddhists, passed to these successor states.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Satavahana dynasty built the first great empire of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>, uniting the western and eastern parts of the peninsula, linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean-trade">Indian Ocean trade</a> with the Roman world, and patronising the Buddhist rock-cut monasteries of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ghats">Western Ghats</a> and the Amaravati stupa while proclaiming a Brahmanical ideal of kingship.</em></p>

